.run/
gates/.trial/
gates/.gates-run-*.sh
# #475: the same thing under the name that run happened to use. A frozen copy of gates.sh is how a suite
# survives a mid-run edit to the repo copy (CLAUDE.md #461, and HANDOFF point 4: the copy must stay INSIDE
# gates/ because gates.sh derives ROOT from dirname $0). It must never be COMMITTED - a second copy of the
# push-gate script is a copy that can silently drift from the one every consumer greps. The established
# name is the pattern above and #475 should have used it.
gates/gates.run.sh

# #497: the measurement probes this build used to find a per-lesson defect. Scratch, never committed:
# a script under gates/ that no suite runs is the thing jobs/a-test-in-the-repo-that-no-suite-runs-2026-09-28
# exists about. The reproducible commands live in TC-R52 instead, where a reader will look for them.
gates/.probe/

# #502: an antagonist subagent wrote a scratch probe to gates/.anta502-probe.js, outside the two
# patterns above, so it showed as untracked and a stop hook asked for it to be committed. A throwaway
# script under gates/ must never enter the tree - that is what
# jobs/a-test-in-the-repo-that-no-suite-runs-2026-09-28 exists about - so the pattern is widened here
# rather than the file committed.
gates/.anta*.js
gates/.probe*.js

# #524, found by antagonist A from the diff door and matching the wrong-reason red antagonist B hit from the
# shipped surface: gates/buildnum.sh:129 is LOCKD="$HERE/.buildnum.lock", a DIRECTORY holding a pid file,
# and it was not ignored by anything above. It is a mutex, never an artefact. While a mint is in flight it is
# an untracked path under gates/, so `git add -A` sweeps it into a commit - which is exactly what
# gates/fastgate-drive.sh's commit_case does when it builds a fixture - and gates/fastgate.sh then sees a
# commit touching an unrecognised gates/ path and exits 2 for "paths are not records-only" instead of the
# reason the case asserts. A measured it inside a live clone. The harness now excludes the path explicitly
# as well; this line is the half that protects every OTHER caller, including a human running `git add -A`
# during a mint, and a stop hook that would otherwise ask for a lock file to be committed.
gates/.buildnum.lock/
